// Fresh Question Bank - Domain 1.0 General Security Concepts
// o = objective, q = question, c = choices, a = index of correct choice, e = explanation for each choice
window.FRESH_BANK = (window.FRESH_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 1.1 Security controls (18) ----------
{o:"1.1",q:"Hours after ransomware encrypted its file servers, Harbor Logistics rebuilt them from last night's offline backup. Which control type does the restore represent?",c:["Corrective control that returns systems to a known-good state","Preventive control that stops the payload from executing","Detective control that identifies the encrypting process in logs","Compensating control that stands in for absent endpoint protection"],a:0,e:["Correct. The incident has already happened and the restore reduces its impact by returning data to a usable state. Limiting damage and recovering afterward is the definition of a corrective control, and backups are the textbook example.","Incorrect. Preventive controls act before an event to block it, for example application allow listing. The encryption has already occurred, so the restore cannot be preventing it. An EDR rule that blocked the payload would be preventive.","Incorrect. Detective controls such as SIEM alerts or log review reveal that something happened. The restore discovers nothing, because the encryption was already known. It would be the answer if the question asked about the alert that flagged the activity.","Incorrect. Compensating controls are alternatives used when the normal control cannot be implemented. Nothing here substitutes for a required safeguard; the backup is simply used in response. It would fit a legacy host that cannot run an EDR agent."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"A hospital cannot install an endpoint agent on a legacy MRI workstation because the vendor would void support. The team puts the workstation on an isolated VLAN with strict ACLs instead. Which control type is the VLAN isolation?",c:["Directive control that instructs staff how to handle the workstation","Compensating control that stands in for the blocked endpoint agent","Detective control that alerts on malware activity on the workstation","Corrective control that cleans the workstation after an infection"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Directive controls are written instructions such as policies and procedures. The VLAN is enforced by network technology, not by telling people what to do. A procedure document for MRI technicians would be directive.","Correct. The preferred control, an endpoint agent, cannot be used, so a different control is applied to give comparable protection. Substituting for a control that cannot be implemented is exactly what compensating means.","Incorrect. Detective controls identify events through logging or alerting. ACLs and segmentation restrict traffic and do not report anything. A network IDS watching the VLAN would be the detective piece.","Incorrect. Corrective controls act after an incident to restore systems. The isolation was put in place in advance and nothing has been infected. Reimaging the workstation after malware would be corrective."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"A retailer posts visible signs on its lot fence saying the property is under 24-hour video surveillance and trespassers will be prosecuted. Some of the cameras are dummies. Which control type are the signs?",c:["Preventive control that physically blocks entry to the lot","Detective control that records who enters the lot","Deterrent control that discourages trespass attempts","Directive control that sets rules for authorized employees"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Preventive controls physically or logically stop the action, such as a locked gate. A sign can be ignored by anyone willing to climb the fence. A gate with a lock would be the preventive choice.","Incorrect. Detective controls capture or notice events, and real cameras are the detective part. Signs record nothing, and the dummy cameras prove the signs work without detecting. A working camera feed would be detective.","Correct. The signs change an attacker's decision by raising the perceived risk of being caught. Because even fake cameras with signs work, the effect is psychological, which is the hallmark of a deterrent.","Incorrect. Directive controls tell people inside the organization what they must do, such as policy. These signs address outsiders and mainly aim to change a would-be intruder's mind. An employee handbook rule would be directive."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Which pairing correctly matches a control with its category?",c:["Host-based firewall configured through Group Policy: Operational","Bollards installed in front of the main lobby: Managerial","Security awareness training delivered by HR: Technical","Quarterly risk assessment led by the CISO: Managerial"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A host firewall is implemented in software and enforced by the system, so it is a technical control. It would be operational only if the item were a person-driven task such as reviewing firewall logs.","Incorrect. Bollards are physical barriers that stop vehicles, so they belong to the physical category. They would be managerial only if the item were a policy about vehicle access.","Incorrect. Training is carried out by people as part of daily activity, so CompTIA treats it as operational. It would be technical only if a system, such as an email filter, enforced it automatically.","Correct. Risk assessments are planning and oversight activities, which makes them managerial. Policies, risk management and security planning are the classic managerial examples."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"An administrator configures the directory service to lock any account for 30 minutes after five failed logins, so password guessing stops before an account is compromised. Which category and type describe this control?",c:["Technical category, corrective type","Operational category, preventive type","Technical category, preventive type","Managerial category, directive type"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The category is right, because the directory enforces it, but corrective controls act after an incident. Lockout intervenes before compromise. Forcing a reset after a confirmed breach would be corrective.","Incorrect. The type is right, but operational controls are performed by people. This setting runs automatically with no analyst involved. A help desk employee manually disabling suspicious accounts would be operational.","Correct. The directory service enforces the rule automatically, which makes it technical, and it blocks guessing before an account falls, which makes it preventive.","Incorrect. A written policy stating that accounts must lock is managerial and directive. The configured setting that actually enforces it is not a document or an instruction to people."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"A SOC analyst manually reviews firewall and VPN logs every morning looking for anomalies. Which category does the daily review fall into?",c:["Operational, because analysts perform it as a recurring task","Managerial, because the findings inform risk decisions","Physical, because the logs describe access to network equipment","Technical, because the data comes from firewall and VPN appliances"],a:0,e:["Correct. Operational controls are day-to-day activities carried out by people, such as log review, backups and configuration management. The analyst, not software, performs this work.","Incorrect. Managerial controls cover planning, policy and oversight. A daily review is routine execution, not governance. A quarterly risk assessment presented to leadership would be managerial.","Incorrect. Physical controls protect facilities and people with locks, barriers and sensors. Reading network logs protects nothing physically. A badge reader at the server room would be physical.","Incorrect. The logging itself is a technical function, but the question is about the human review. A SIEM correlation rule that processes the logs automatically would be technical."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Northwind Credit Union publishes a written acceptable use policy that every employee signs. Nothing technically enforces it. Which control type is the policy?",c:["Compensating control that replaces a missing technical safeguard","Preventive control that technically stops prohibited actions","Detective control that discovers violations of the rule","Directive control that tells employees what they must do"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Compensating controls cover for a control that cannot be implemented. The policy is not standing in for anything missing; it is the primary directive. It would apply if a technical control were infeasible and a procedure replaced it.","Incorrect. A preventive control stops the action, and nothing here blocks anyone. A web filter that blocks prohibited sites would be preventive. The signed policy only instructs.","Incorrect. Detective controls find violations through monitoring or audit. The policy neither monitors nor reports anything. DLP alerts on prohibited uploads would be detective.","Correct. Directive controls tell people what they must or must not do. Policies, standards and procedures are directive, and this one works through instruction alone."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Which of the following is a purely detective control?",c:["Access control vestibule at the data center door","Network IDS in passive mode on a SPAN port","Logon banner stating that access is monitored","BitLocker full-disk encryption on laptops"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A vestibule stops tailgating by letting one person through at a time, which makes it preventive. It would be detective only if it merely logged entries.","Correct. A passive IDS on a SPAN port observes copies of traffic and alerts, but it cannot block anything. Identifying without stopping is what detective means; an inline IPS would be preventive.","Incorrect. A banner tries to discourage misuse, so it is deterrent. It does not detect anything; monitoring tools do that. It would be correct if the question asked for a deterrent.","Incorrect. Encryption prevents disclosure if a laptop is lost. It does not notice or report anything. It is preventive, and arguably also a data protection control."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Visitors at a company receive bright orange badges, but no lock or turnstile stops anyone from walking in. The badges mainly help employees notice an unescorted stranger. Which control type is that function?",c:["Preventive control that physically stops entry to the building","Detective control that helps staff notice a stranger","Corrective control that removes an intruder after discovery","Compensating control that replaces a missing door lock"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Preventive controls physically stop access, and the stem says nothing blocks entry. A badge reader on the door would be preventive. The badge only makes people easy to spot.","Correct. The badge makes unauthorized people stand out so staff can identify them. Noticing a problem without stopping it is a detective function.","Incorrect. Corrective controls limit damage after an event, such as escorting out and documenting an intruder. The badge helps with noticing, which comes before any response.","Incorrect. A compensating control substitutes for a control that cannot be used. The scenario describes no missing lock that cannot be installed, so the badge is not replacing one."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"After a breach, the team adds a signature to its inline IPS, running in blocking mode, that drops traffic matching the exploit that was used. Which control type is the new signature?",c:["Deterrent control that warns attackers they are being watched","Corrective control that repairs hosts already compromised","Detective control that logs matches for later analyst review","Preventive control that drops the exploit before it lands"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Deterrents discourage attackers through visible warnings, and attackers do not see IPS signatures. A banner on the VPN login page would be deterrent.","Incorrect. Corrective controls repair or restore systems already affected, such as reimaging. The signature repairs nothing; it blocks future attempts. Restoring the breached server would be corrective.","Incorrect. A detect-only signature that logs matches would be detective. The stem states the IPS is in blocking mode, so it acts instead of just recording.","Correct. Although it was added because of the breach, the signature's function going forward is to block the exploit before it reaches any host. Stopping an action in the data path is preventive."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Which control is BOTH physical in category and preventive in type?",c:["Keyed lock on the server rack cage door","Sign-in sheet at the reception desk","Warning poster about tailgating in the lobby","Dome cameras covering the server row"],a:0,e:["Correct. A lock is a physical object that stops unauthorized access outright. Physical category plus a blocking function equals preventive.","Incorrect. A sign-in sheet records who visited, so it is detective (and operational). It does not prevent anyone from entering.","Incorrect. A poster is physical but deterrent, because it only reminds people not to tailgate. A mantrap would be the physical preventive version.","Incorrect. Cameras are physical but detective, since they record events and stop nobody. They would be the answer if the question asked for physical and detective."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"A company is required to encrypt cardholder data at rest, but one legacy database cannot support encryption. The team adds tokenization in front of it and stricter monitoring. What term describes the added measures?",c:["Corrective controls that repair damage from an earlier incident","Directive controls that document how staff must handle card data","Compensating controls that offset the unmet encryption requirement","Deterrent controls that discourage staff from querying card data"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Corrective controls act after an incident. No breach has occurred here. Restoring card data after a compromise would be corrective.","Incorrect. Directive controls are policies and procedures. Tokenization and monitoring are technical measures, not instructions. A written card-handling procedure would be directive.","Correct. When a requirement such as encryption cannot be met as written, alternative measures that provide equivalent protection are compensating controls. PCI DSS formally allows this approach.","Incorrect. Deterrent controls discourage by raising perceived risk, such as warning banners. Tokenization removes real card data from exposure, so it does more than discourage."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Which is an example of a managerial control?",c:["Enforcing TLS 1.3 only on the public web servers","Contracting an armed guard for the lobby desk","Reviewing badge access logs each week for anomalies","Approving a documented change management policy"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Configuring protocols is implemented in technology, so it is technical. It would be managerial only if it were a written standard requiring TLS.","Incorrect. A guard is a physical control, with operational duties. Hiring staff to protect a space is not governance.","Incorrect. Reviewing logs is a recurring task carried out by people, so it is operational. A policy requiring weekly reviews would be the managerial piece.","Correct. Policies, governance and formal processes direct and oversee security, which is the managerial category. Change management policy is a classic example."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Nightingale Pharmacy's written backup policy requires full backups every Sunday night. How is the written POLICY itself (not the backup jobs) classified?",c:["Managerial category, preventive type","Operational category, corrective type","Managerial category, directive type","Technical category, directive type"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The category is right, but a policy does not technically stop anything, so it is not preventive. A permission setting that blocks deletion of backups would be preventive.","Incorrect. Running backups is operational and restoring is corrective, but the question asks about the policy document, not the jobs or restores.","Correct. A written policy is part of governance, so it is managerial, and it tells staff what must happen, so it is directive.","Incorrect. The type is right, but a document is not a technical control. Technical controls are systems and settings, such as the backup software schedule."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"A SIEM rule detects an impossible-travel login and automatically disables the account. Which control type BEST describes the automatic disable action itself, not the detection?",c:["Detective control that identifies the impossible-travel pattern","Corrective control that limits damage from a login in progress","Directive control that instructs users how to sign in","Deterrent control that discourages credential theft"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The detection of the pattern is the detective part, but the question asks about the action taken afterward. An alert-only rule would be detective.","Correct. The suspicious event is already happening and the automation contains it by cutting off access. Responding to limit impact is a corrective function.","Incorrect. Directive controls are instructions to people. The disable is carried out by software without any person being told what to do.","Incorrect. Deterrents discourage attackers before they act, such as visible monitoring warnings. An automated disable happens after a suspicious login and does not influence intent."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Which is the BEST example of an operational control?",c:["Annual phishing awareness sessions run by HR","NGFW inspecting all inbound traffic at the network edge","Retina scanner at the data center door","Board-approved statement of risk appetite"],a:0,e:["Correct. Awareness programs are carried out by people as part of ongoing operations, and CompTIA lists training as operational.","Incorrect. A next-generation firewall is implemented in technology, so it is technical. A person reviewing its logs daily would be operational.","Incorrect. A retina scanner is a technical biometric control, even though it protects a physical space. A guard checking IDs would be operational.","Incorrect. A statement of risk appetite from the board sets direction and governance, which is managerial."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Motion-activated floodlights are installed around a warehouse yard where there are no cameras or alarms. What is their MAIN control type?",c:["Deterrent control that makes intruders feel exposed","Preventive control that physically blocks entry to the yard","Corrective control that restores damaged property","Directive control that sets rules for site staff"],a:0,e:["Correct. Sudden lighting makes intruders feel visible and discourages them from continuing. With no cameras to detect, the lights act as a physical deterrent.","Incorrect. Preventive controls block access, like fences and locks. Lights do not stop anyone from climbing in. A locked gate would be preventive.","Incorrect. Corrective controls repair or restore after an incident. Lights do not fix anything after the fact. Replacing a stolen asset from insurance would be corrective.","Incorrect. Directive controls are written instructions. A floodlight issues no instruction. A site policy telling staff to lock the yard would be directive."]},
{o:"1.1",q:"Which statement about control categories and types is accurate?",c:["Category is what a control does, and type is how it is implemented","Category is how a control is built; a control can serve several types","Each control has exactly one type, so a guard cannot deter and detect","Physical controls cannot be preventive because they depend on people"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the terms. Category (technical, managerial, operational, physical) describes how a control is implemented, and type describes its function.","Correct. Category covers implementation (technical, managerial, operational, physical) and type covers function (preventive, detective, corrective, deterrent, compensating, directive). Many controls span several types.","Incorrect. A single control often serves multiple types. A guard deters by being visible, detects by watching and prevents by blocking entry.","Incorrect. Locks, fences and bollards are physical and preventive, and none depends on a person acting in the moment."]},
// ---------- 1.2 Fundamental concepts (24) ----------
{o:"1.2",q:"An attacker on the path between two banks changes the amount in a wire transfer request. The transfer completes on time, and the attacker never needs to keep a copy of the contents. Which security property was MOST directly violated?",c:["Availability, because the request was delayed before processing","Confidentiality, because the request was exposed to a third party","Integrity, because the request content was modified in transit","Non-repudiation, because the sender could later deny the request"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Availability concerns access to systems and data. The transfer completed on time, so nothing was delayed or denied. A DDoS against the banking portal would violate availability.","Incorrect. Confidentiality concerns unauthorized disclosure. The attacker may have read the request, but the stem focuses on the change, not on keeping or exposing contents. It would fit if the scenario were about eavesdropping only.","Correct. Altering the amount means the data no longer matches what the sender produced, which is a loss of integrity. Hashing, HMACs and digital signatures are the matching safeguards.","Incorrect. Non-repudiation is about proving who sent something and is not part of the CIA triad. The sender is not disputing anything here. It would apply to a signed request that the sender later denied."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"A sender digitally signs a contract but does not encrypt it. Which property does the signature provide that prevents her from later denying she sent it?",c:["Confidentiality, because the contract text is hidden from outsiders","Authorization, because the signer's permissions are checked","Accounting, because signing creates a log of the signer's actions","Non-repudiation, because only the private key holder could sign"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The contract is not encrypted, so anyone can read it. Confidentiality would require encrypting it with the recipient's public key or a session key.","Incorrect. Authorization decides what an authenticated identity may do. A signature does not check the signer's permissions. It would apply if the system decided whether she may approve contracts.","Incorrect. Accounting records activity in logs. A signature is a cryptographic proof attached to the document, not a record kept by a system. Audit logging would provide accounting.","Correct. A signature can be generated only with the signer's private key, so she cannot credibly deny it. That binding of an action to a person is non-repudiation."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"A RADIUS server stores an entry noting that jsmith opened the payroll share at 02:14. Which AAA function produced that entry?",c:["Authentication, which proves the identity jsmith claims","Authorization, which decides what jsmith may access","Accounting, which keeps an audit trail of user activity","Attestation, which formally certifies that access is appropriate"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Authentication happened when jsmith logged in, and it checks identity, not activity afterward. It would be the answer if the entry described a successful password and MFA check.","Incorrect. Authorization decides whether jsmith is permitted to open the share. The entry records that it happened, not that a decision was made. An ACL lookup would be authorization.","Correct. Accounting tracks who did what and when, producing logs and audit trails. RADIUS and TACACS+ both include accounting.","Incorrect. Attestation is a formal statement or review confirming access is appropriate, such as a periodic access review. It is not part of AAA and does not log individual events."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Before any user signs in, a VPN gateway validates a certificate installed on the laptop. What is being authenticated at that step?",c:["The device, through its machine certificate","The session, through a token issued after login completes","The application, through an API key embedded in the client","The user, through a password entered at the login prompt"],a:0,e:["Correct. A machine certificate proves the device is a trusted, enrolled system. This is common with 802.1X and always-on VPNs and happens before user authentication.","Incorrect. A session token is created after authentication succeeds, so it cannot be what is checked first. Token validation would apply to later requests.","Incorrect. API keys identify applications or services calling an API. The check here uses a certificate tied to the machine, not a key embedded in client software.","Incorrect. The user authenticates afterward with credentials. At this step nobody has signed in yet, so the user cannot be what is being verified. It would apply to an MFA prompt."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Within the Zero Trust control plane, which component evaluates an access request and returns the grant-or-deny verdict that other components carry out?",c:["Policy enforcement point, which sits in the data path","Policy administrator, which relays the decision to the PEP","Subject, which presents credentials with the request","Policy engine, which weighs trust signals against policy"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The PEP enforces the decision by opening or closing the connection but does not decide. It is in the data plane, not the control plane.","Incorrect. The administrator takes the engine's verdict and configures the PEP, so it carries out the decision rather than making it. It would be correct for the component that establishes the session path.","Incorrect. The subject is the user or system making the request. It supplies information used in the decision but does not decide.","Correct. The policy engine evaluates the request against policy and signals such as identity, device posture and threat intelligence, then issues the decision that the administrator and PEP act on."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Which Zero Trust component operates in the data plane rather than the control plane?",c:["Policy engine, evaluating trust signals for each request","Policy enforcement point, gating each session's traffic","Policy administrator, issuing session instructions to the PEP","Adaptive identity, tuning authentication by context"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The policy engine is the decision maker and belongs to the control plane. It would be the answer if the question asked where the grant-or-deny decision is made.","Correct. The PEP sits in the path of the traffic and carries out the decisions made in the control plane, opening or closing the connection.","Incorrect. The policy administrator is a control plane component that tells the PEP to allow or tear down a session. It does not handle user traffic.","Incorrect. Adaptive identity is a control-plane concept that adjusts authentication based on context. It is not a component that sits in the data path."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Stonebridge Insurance wants step-up MFA triggered when an employee signs in from a new country on an unmanaged device. Which Zero Trust concept does this describe?",c:["Implicit trust zone, which trusts traffic originating inside it","Adaptive identity, which adjusts authentication to context signals","Policy-driven access control, which grants access by role defined in policy","Threat scope reduction, which shrinks what each account can reach"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Implicit trust zones are areas where traffic is trusted by default, which Zero Trust tries to eliminate. The scenario does the opposite by challenging a risky login.","Correct. Adaptive identity varies authentication requirements using signals such as location, device state and behavior, which is exactly what step-up MFA for a risky login does.","Incorrect. Policy-driven access control relies on role or attribute rules written in policy. The stem asks for a stronger challenge triggered by changing context, which a static role mapping does not provide.","Incorrect. Threat scope reduction limits what an attacker or account can reach, for example through least privilege and segmentation. It does not vary the login challenge. It would fit a question about microsegmentation."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Which statement BEST describes the Zero Trust model?",c:["Trust internal users after perimeter authentication succeeds","Trust devices that pass a one-time NAC posture check","Place sensitive systems inside a hardened DMZ perimeter","Verify every request, wherever on the network it originates"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Trusting anyone because they are inside the network is the perimeter model that Zero Trust replaces. It would describe a traditional castle-and-moat design.","Incorrect. A single NAC check followed by lasting trust is implicit trust. Zero Trust re-evaluates trust continuously as context changes.","Incorrect. A DMZ is a perimeter-based segmentation technique. It limits exposure of public services but does not verify each request.","Correct. Zero Trust assumes no implicit trust. Every request is authenticated, authorized and evaluated in context, whether it comes from inside or outside the network."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"An analyst performs a gap analysis against ISO 27001. What is its main output?",c:["Comparison of current posture to a desired standard","Ranking of likely threat actors by capability and motivation","Timeline of attacker actions reconstructed after an incident","Scan-driven list of unpatched vulnerabilities on every host"],a:0,e:["Correct. A gap analysis compares where the organization is now with where a target framework says it should be, then lists the missing controls or practices.","Incorrect. Ranking threat actors is threat intelligence or threat modeling. It does not measure compliance against a framework.","Incorrect. A reconstructed timeline is an incident response or forensic product, created after an event rather than to measure framework conformance.","Incorrect. A list of unpatched hosts comes from a vulnerability scan. A gap analysis is broader, covering controls, policies and processes. It would be the answer for a vulnerability assessment."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Quarry Tech saves a spreadsheet named Q4_Salaries.xlsx on a shared drive. Nobody has a legitimate reason to open it, and any access to it triggers an alert. What is it?",c:["Honeypot, a decoy system that attracts attackers","Honeytoken, fake data such as a credential or record that signals misuse","Honeyfile, a bait document placed among real files","Honeynet, a network of decoy systems that simulates an environment"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A honeypot is an entire decoy system such as a fake server. A single document on a real file share is not a system. It would apply to a fake SSH server.","Incorrect. A honeytoken is planted data such as a fake credential, API key or database row. The scenario is a whole decoy document, which has its own name.","Correct. A honeyfile is an enticing decoy document that alerts when touched. It is useful for catching insiders or intruders browsing shares.","Incorrect. A honeynet is multiple honeypots forming a decoy network. One file is far smaller in scope."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"A security team embeds a fake AWS access key in an internal configuration file. Any attempt to authenticate with that key sends an alert. What is this technique?",c:["Honeyfile, a bait document placed among real files","Honeypot, a decoy system that attracts attackers","DNS sinkhole, which redirects lookups of malicious domains","Honeytoken, fake data that signals misuse when used"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A honeyfile is the decoy document itself. Here the config file is real and only the key inside it is fake. It would be correct if an entire fake document were the bait.","Incorrect. A honeypot is a decoy system that attackers interact with. Nothing is hosting a service here; the bait is a single piece of data.","Incorrect. A DNS sinkhole returns controlled answers for malicious domains to block or analyze callbacks. It does not involve a planted credential.","Correct. A honeytoken is fabricated data such as credentials, keys or records with no legitimate use. Any use of it signals compromise."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"A research lab deploys several interconnected decoy servers, a fake domain controller and fake workstations to study how attackers move laterally. What have they built?",c:["Sandbox, an isolated space for detonating suspicious files","Honeypot, a single decoy system that lures attackers","Honeynet, a network of honeypots imitating an environment","Honeyfile, a bait document placed among real files"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A sandbox executes suspicious files in isolation to observe their behavior. It does not lure attackers across a network.","Incorrect. A honeypot is one decoy system. This design has multiple interconnected systems, which is a larger construct. A single fake web server would be a honeypot.","Correct. A honeynet is a network of honeypots that imitates a realistic environment, which allows researchers to watch movement between systems.","Incorrect. A honeyfile is a single decoy document. It cannot show lateral movement between hosts."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Which physical control BEST stops an unauthorized person from following an authorized employee through an entry door?",c:["Badge reader with PIN on the entry door","Access vestibule with interlocking doors","Motion-activated lighting on the approach path","CCTV with recording at the entry point"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A badge and PIN verify the person who badges in, but the door still opens for whoever walks through behind them. It would be right if the question were about verifying identity.","Correct. A vestibule has two interlocking doors so only one person can pass at a time, which physically prevents tailgating.","Incorrect. Lighting deters and aids detection but does not block the door. It would answer a question about discouraging intruders at night.","Incorrect. Cameras record tailgating after the fact, which makes them detective. They do not physically stop anyone."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"A data center uses sensors that detect the thermal signature of a person to trigger an alarm after hours. Which sensor type is this?",c:["Infrared sensor detecting radiated warmth","Microwave sensor detecting reflected radio waves","Ultrasonic sensor detecting changes in reflected sound","Pressure sensor detecting weight on a floor mat"],a:0,e:["Correct. Infrared (often PIR) sensors detect the heat that bodies radiate, which matches a thermal signature trigger.","Incorrect. Microwave sensors emit radio waves and detect motion from the reflections. They are not based on heat.","Incorrect. Ultrasonic sensors emit sound above human hearing and detect motion from changes in the echo. They do not measure temperature.","Incorrect. Pressure sensors react to weight on mats or floors. They do not sense heat. They would fit a question about detecting footsteps on a raised floor."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Which barrier is designed to stop a vehicle from ramming a building entrance while still allowing pedestrians to walk through?",c:["Steel bollards set along the curb line","Vestibule with interlocking doors inside the lobby","Chain-link fencing along the property line","Turnstile that admits one badge-holder at a time"],a:0,e:["Correct. Bollards are short, rugged posts that block vehicles while leaving gaps that pedestrians can pass through.","Incorrect. A vestibule controls the flow of people inside the building and does nothing to stop a vehicle.","Incorrect. Fencing stops pedestrians as well as vehicles and is not rated for impact. It would fit a question about perimeter demarcation.","Incorrect. A turnstile controls individual people. It would not stop a vehicle and is not designed for impact."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Which CIA properties does change management MAINLY support?",c:["Confidentiality and integrity, by encrypting configuration files in transit","Availability and integrity, by controlling untested, unapproved changes","Confidentiality and availability, by replicating data to a secondary site","Non-repudiation and confidentiality, by signing every change request"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Encrypting configuration files protects confidentiality but is a cryptographic control, not what change management does. Change management is a process.","Correct. Approved, tested and documented changes reduce outages (availability) and unauthorized or incorrect modifications (integrity).","Incorrect. Replication supports availability and disaster recovery. It is a resilience technique, not change management.","Incorrect. Digital signatures can strengthen change records, but non-repudiation is not part of the CIA triad and signing is not the core of change management."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Which of the following BEST describes authorization?",c:["Proving that a claimed identity is genuine","Recording the actions an identity performed on a system","Determining what an authenticated identity may do","Claiming an identity by supplying a username"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Proving an identity is genuine is authentication. It happens before authorization.","Incorrect. Recording actions is accounting, which produces logs and audit trails.","Correct. Authorization comes after authentication and determines the permissions, such as read or write, that the identity has based on roles, attributes or policy.","Incorrect. Supplying a username is identification, the claim that authentication then verifies."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Lakeside Clinic's help desk resets a password after the caller states an employee ID. The reset is logged, and the help desk role is allowed to reset passwords. An attacker who found an employee ID online takes over an account. Which part of the process is the weakest link?",c:["Authorization, because help desk staff can reset any account","Identification, because the caller never stated who they were","Accounting, because the reset was not recorded for review","Authentication, because the ID was accepted as proof"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Resetting passwords is a legitimate permission for the help desk role, and the stem confirms it is allowed. The failure was in confirming who was asking, not in what the role may do.","Incorrect. The caller did claim an identity by giving an employee ID. Identification occurred, but the claim was never verified.","Incorrect. The stem states the reset was logged. Accounting worked, but a log entry does not stop the takeover.","Correct. An employee ID is not secret, so accepting it as proof means identity was never properly verified. A callback, MFA push or manager approval would fix it."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"A Zero Trust architect applies threat scope reduction. Which action fits?",c:["Adding step-up MFA when a login looks risky","Placing an enforcement point in front of each application","Restricting each workload to the resources it requires","Logging every authorization decision to a central SIEM"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Triggering stronger authentication based on risk is adaptive identity. It would be the answer if the question asked about context-based authentication.","Incorrect. Placing a PEP in front of applications is part of the architecture that enforces decisions. It enforces policy but does not by itself narrow what a subject is allowed to reach.","Correct. Threat scope reduction limits what any one account or workload can reach, through least privilege and microsegmentation, so a compromise has a smaller blast radius.","Incorrect. Central logging supports detection and auditing. It does not reduce what an attacker can reach."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Which BEST describes a security zone in network or Zero Trust design?",c:["Logical grouping of assets with similar trust requirements","Approved list of applications permitted to run on endpoints","Physical room where servers are housed and badge-controlled","Geographic cloud region hosting a tenant's workloads"],a:0,e:["Correct. Security zones group assets by trust level and sensitivity, such as DMZ, internal and restricted, so policy can control traffic between zones.","Incorrect. A list of permitted applications is an allow list. It governs software, not network trust boundaries.","Incorrect. Zones can map to physical locations but are defined by trust level, not by a room. A badge-controlled server room is a physical control.","Incorrect. A cloud region is a geographic hosting location. It affects latency and data residency, not trust grouping."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Auditors need proof that a specific employee, and not someone else, approved a purchase order in the ERP system. Which combination BEST supports this?",c:["Role-based approver group with a rotated shared password","Nightly backups of the ERP database","Encryption at rest for the ERP database","Individual accounts with MFA plus audit logging"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A shared password means anyone in the group could have approved, so actions cannot be tied to one person. Rotation reduces exposure but does not create accountability.","Incorrect. Backups support availability and recovery. They do not attribute actions to individuals.","Incorrect. Encryption at rest protects stored data from disclosure. It says nothing about who performed an action.","Correct. Unique accounts with strong authentication tie each approval to one person, and audit logs record it. Together they support accountability and non-repudiation."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"Motion sensors, cameras, badge readers and guards are arranged in layers around a data center. Which principle does this show?",c:["Least privilege, granting only the access a job needs","Defense in depth, so one failed control is backed by others","Separation of duties, splitting a sensitive task among people","Security through obscurity, hiding details of the design"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Least privilege limits access rights for a user or process. The scenario describes multiple controls overlapping, not rights being restricted.","Correct. Layering several different controls means that if one fails, others still protect the asset. That is defense in depth.","Incorrect. Separation of duties prevents one person from completing a sensitive task alone. It concerns task assignment, not overlapping safeguards.","Incorrect. Security through obscurity relies on secrecy of the design. These controls are visible and do not depend on secrecy."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"In Zero Trust, the policy administrator's role is BEST described as:",c:["Weighing trust signals to decide whether access is granted","Telling the PEP to open or close the session path","Enforcing the decision directly in the traffic path","Storing the directory of user accounts and credentials"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Weighing signals and deciding is the policy engine's job. The administrator acts on that decision.","Correct. The policy administrator takes the engine's decision and instructs the enforcement point to open or close the communication path, including issuing session credentials.","Incorrect. Enforcing in the traffic path is the PEP's role, in the data plane. The administrator is in the control plane.","Incorrect. Account and credential storage is the job of a directory or identity provider, which is a data source for the decision rather than the administrator."]},
{o:"1.2",q:"A lobby receptionist checks visitors in, issues escorted-visitor badges and records arrival times in a log. Which control type is the visitor LOG itself?",c:["Preventive control that stops unauthorized people from entering","Corrective control that restores access after a lockout","Compensating control that replaces a missing badge reader","Detective control that creates a record of who was on site"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The log stops nobody. Preventive effect comes from the receptionist and escort rules. A locked door would be preventive.","Incorrect. Corrective controls limit damage or restore operations after an event. A log restores nothing.","Incorrect. The log is not standing in for a control that cannot be installed. It would be compensating only if it replaced something like a badge reader that was infeasible.","Correct. A log creates evidence that can reveal who was present during an incident. Recording and reviewing events is a detective function."]},
// ---------- 1.3 Change management (10) ----------
{o:"1.3",q:"An engineer wants to add a firewall rule in production. Which step comes FIRST in a formal change management process?",c:["Submit a request stating the reason, impact and backout plan","Test the rule in a staging environment that mirrors production","Update the network diagrams and configuration baselines","Schedule a maintenance window with affected users"],a:0,e:["Correct. The process starts with a documented request describing what, why, the expected impact and how to back out, so reviewers can approve or reject it.","Incorrect. Testing happens after the change is documented and reviewed, not before anyone has formally requested it. It would be the answer if the request were already approved and the question asked about validation.","Incorrect. Documentation is updated after implementation to reflect the new state. Doing it first would record something that has not happened and may never be approved.","Incorrect. Scheduling depends on approval and on understanding impact, so it follows the request. It would be the next step once the change is approved."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"Which group typically weighs the risk and timing of significant changes and approves them before implementation?",c:["Red team, which simulates adversary attacks","Incident response team, which contains and eradicates active incidents","Change Advisory Board (CAB), which weighs impact and timing","Data custodians, which manage storage and backups"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The red team performs authorized attack simulations to test defenses. It does not approve changes.","Incorrect. The incident response team handles security incidents as they occur, not planned changes. It might be involved if the change were an emergency fix to an active incident.","Correct. The CAB reviews proposed changes for risk, impact and scheduling and then approves or rejects them.","Incorrect. Data custodians handle the technical upkeep of data, such as storage and backups. They carry out tasks rather than approve organization-wide changes."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"During a scheduled window, a database patch fails and corrupts records. Which element of the change plan should the team use NOW?",c:["Impact analysis, which predicts effects before the change","Stakeholder analysis, which identifies who is affected","Maintenance window, which defines when changes are allowed","Backout plan, which returns systems to the prior state"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Impact analysis was done earlier to predict consequences. It does not repair a failure that already happened.","Incorrect. Stakeholder analysis identifies who needs to be informed or consulted. It is useful for communication, not for recovery.","Incorrect. The maintenance window is only the scheduled time slot. Being inside the window does not undo the failure.","Correct. A backout (rollback) plan documents how to restore the previous working state if a change fails, which is what the team needs now."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"What is the purpose of a maintenance window in change management?",c:["To allow emergency changes without documented approval","To replace pre-production testing with live validation","To run approved changes at low-impact times","To limit patching to vendor-defined release dates"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Emergency changes have their own expedited approval process. A maintenance window does not remove the need for approval.","Incorrect. Testing still occurs in a test or staging environment before production. A window is about timing, not validation.","Correct. Scheduling approved changes for low-usage periods limits the availability impact if something goes wrong.","Incorrect. Vendor release schedules, like monthly patch days, influence what is available but do not define a maintenance window. Windows are chosen by the business based on impact."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"After a switch firmware upgrade, the team revises network diagrams and configuration baselines. Which change management activity is this?",c:["Performing impact analysis of the upgrade's effects","Updating documents and baselines for the new state","Running test cases to validate the upgrade works","Obtaining CAB approval for the implementation"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Impact analysis happens before the change to anticipate consequences. Updating diagrams afterward is not prediction.","Correct. Diagrams, baselines and procedures must reflect the current state. Without this, the next engineer works from wrong information.","Incorrect. Testing validates that the change works. It does not record the new configuration. It would be correct if the team were executing validation steps.","Incorrect. Approval is obtained before implementation. These updates occur after the upgrade completes."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"A developer asks to add a new application to the firewall allow list. The firewall is default-deny, and the application also talks to a database on a different port. Which technical implication matters MOST for the change?",c:["Dependent connections, like the database port, must be allowed","Incompatibility with an unsupported operating system","Downtime from restarting the firewall service during the change","Stale network diagrams that no longer match the topology"],a:0,e:["Correct. Under default-deny, the application fails unless its dependent ports and services are also allowed. Each added entry also increases exposure, which is why allow list changes go through change management.","Incorrect. An unsupported OS is a legacy dependency issue. Nothing in the stem suggests the OS is a problem.","Incorrect. Many firewalls apply rule changes without a restart, and the stem does not mention one. A restart would matter if the change required service downtime.","Incorrect. Documentation drift is a real follow-up task, but it is not a technical blocker for the application working. It would matter more after the change is complete."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"A patch on a payment gateway requires restarting the service, during which transactions cannot be processed. Which technical implication must the change plan address?",c:["Downtime that needs a scheduled maintenance window","Incompatibility with an unsupported legacy dependency","A larger attack surface from newly opened ports","Stale network diagrams that no longer match the topology"],a:0,e:["Correct. A required restart means transactions stop, so the plan needs a scheduled window, notifications and possibly redundancy to protect availability.","Incorrect. Legacy dependencies matter when old software blocks patching. The stem describes a restart, not an unsupported component.","Incorrect. Opening ports expands attack surface, but the patch does not add any. It would be the issue for an allow list or firewall change.","Incorrect. Documentation drift is a follow-up task. The stem emphasizes the restart and transaction impact."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"An approved change to a payroll system must be validated before go-live without risking real employees' pay data. Where should testing occur?",c:["In production during off-hours with a rollback ready","In a staging environment configured to mirror production","On a developer's laptop using sample data","In an isolated sandbox with a minimal default configuration"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Off-hours testing still exposes real data and systems to the change. A rollback helps but does not prevent a failure from affecting pay data.","Correct. Staging that mirrors production reveals configuration and compatibility problems without touching live data.","Incorrect. A laptop with sample data does not reproduce the server configuration, integrations or scale of production. It is acceptable for early development testing.","Incorrect. A bare sandbox lacks the production configuration, so passing there does not show the change will work. It suits malware analysis or isolated experiments."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"A legacy application runs only on Windows Server 2008 and breaks if the OS is patched. Management cannot retire it this year. Which approach fits change management?",c:["Excepting the host from change management entirely","Applying the patch anyway and fixing breakage afterward","Isolating the host with compensating controls","Decommissioning the application as soon as possible"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. No system is exempt from change management. Legacy hosts need it more because changes carry higher risk.","Incorrect. Patching without testing risks an outage and skips approval and backout planning. It could be acceptable if testing showed the app survives the patch.","Correct. When a legacy dependency prevents patching, the change process documents the risk and applies compensating controls such as network isolation and monitoring.","Incorrect. Retirement is ideal long-term but the stem says management cannot retire it this year. The immediate need is to manage risk."]},
{o:"1.3",q:"In change management, who is the change OWNER?",c:["Group of people and teams affected by the change","Chair of the board that reviews and approves changes","Vendor representative who supplied the update","Person accountable for the change and its outcome"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. People affected by a change are stakeholders. They are consulted or informed but do not own the change.","Incorrect. The board chair leads the approval process but does not own each individual change.","Incorrect. A vendor may supply software or advice, but accountability for an internal change rests with the organization.","Correct. The owner sponsors the request, is accountable for it and ensures it is carried through to completion."]},
// ---------- 1.4 Cryptographic solutions (20) ----------
{o:"1.4",q:"Alice wants to send Bob a message that only Bob can read, using asymmetric encryption. Which key should she use to encrypt it?",c:["Alice's private key, so anyone with her public key can decrypt","Alice's public key, so only Alice's private key can decrypt","Bob's public key, so only Bob's private key can decrypt","Bob's private key, so only Bob can produce the ciphertext"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Encrypting with a private key yields something anyone holding the public key can decrypt, which proves origin but gives no confidentiality. It describes how a digital signature works.","Incorrect. Only Alice's private key could decrypt that, so Bob could not read it. It would be right only if Alice were storing a message for herself.","Correct. Encrypting with the recipient's public key means only the matching private key, which only Bob holds, can decrypt the message.","Incorrect. Alice never has Bob's private key, and encrypting with it would not keep the message secret from anyone with Bob's public key."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Bob wants recipients to be able to verify that a document came from him and was not altered. What should he do?",c:["Hash the document and encrypt the hash with his private key","Encrypt the whole document with Alice's public key and send it","Encrypt the document with a shared symmetric key from a vault","Add a random salt and hash the document repeatedly"],a:0,e:["Correct. A digital signature is a hash encrypted with the signer's private key. Anyone with Bob's public key can verify who signed it and that it was not changed.","Incorrect. This keeps the document secret for Alice, but anyone can encrypt with her public key, so it proves nothing about the sender. It fits a confidentiality requirement.","Incorrect. A shared key is held by more than one party, so it cannot prove which party created the document. It would suit bulk encryption between two trusted systems.","Incorrect. Salting and repeated hashing belong to password storage. They produce no proof of who created the document because there is no private key."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"A company wants to store user passwords so that no one, including administrators, can recover the plaintext and offline cracking after a database theft is slow. Which approach is BEST?",c:["Encrypt passwords with AES-256 and store the key in an HSM","Hash passwords with SHA-256 using one site-wide salt","Hash passwords with unsalted MD5 for fast verification","Hash with a unique salt per user using bcrypt or PBKDF2"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Encryption is reversible, so anyone with access to the key, including administrators, can recover plaintext. It would suit data that must be read later.","Incorrect. A single shared salt gives identical passwords identical hashes and SHA-256 is fast, which makes brute force cheap. Per-user salts and a slow algorithm are needed.","Incorrect. MD5 is fast and broken, and without salt it is easily cracked with precomputed tables. Speed is exactly what a password hash should avoid.","Correct. Unique salts defeat rainbow tables and hide duplicate passwords, and slow algorithms like bcrypt and PBKDF2 make each guess expensive. Hashing is one-way."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"What does a salt do in password hashing?",c:["Repeats the hash thousands of times to slow guessing","Adds unique random data so identical passwords differ","Appends a secret key that is stored apart from the hashes","Encrypts the hash so only the server can read it"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Repeating the hash is key stretching, as in PBKDF2. A salt is random data and is not an iteration count.","Correct. A unique salt per password makes identical passwords produce different hashes and makes precomputed rainbow tables useless.","Incorrect. A secret value stored separately from the hashes is a pepper. A salt is not secret and is stored alongside the hash.","Incorrect. A salt does not encrypt anything, and the hash remains a one-way value. Encrypting hashes would be a separate protection."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Which of the following uses a SYMMETRIC algorithm?",c:["RSA-2048 for encrypting small payloads","AES-256 for bulk file encryption","ECDSA with the P-256 curve for signing","Diffie-Hellman group 14 for key agreement"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. RSA is asymmetric, using a public and private key pair. It is typically used to protect small items such as keys.","Correct. AES uses the same key to encrypt and decrypt, and it is fast enough for bulk data.","Incorrect. ECDSA is an asymmetric signature algorithm based on elliptic curves.","Incorrect. Diffie-Hellman is an asymmetric key agreement method. It produces a shared secret but does not encrypt data directly."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Two servers must agree on a shared session key across an untrusted network without the key ever being transmitted. Which technique fits?",c:["RSA key transport, encrypting a random key under the server's public key","Key escrow, storing a copy of the key with a trusted third party","A SHA-256 hash of a passphrase that both administrators memorize","Diffie-Hellman exchange that derives the same secret on both sides"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. RSA key transport sends the session key itself, encrypted. It works, but the stem says the key must never be transmitted. It would fit if encrypted transmission were acceptable.","Incorrect. Escrow stores recovery copies of keys with a third party. It does not establish a session key between two parties.","Incorrect. A hash of a memorized passphrase is a static pre-shared secret, not a negotiated session key, and it would be reused and guessable.","Correct. Each side contributes a value and combines it with the other's public value to compute the same secret, which never crosses the network. This is used in TLS and IPsec."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Which PKI component revokes certificates and publishes the list of revoked serial numbers?",c:["Certificate authority, which publishes a CRL","Key escrow agent, which holds recovery copies of keys","Subscriber, which submits a CSR for its certificate","Registration authority, which verifies identity for the CA"],a:0,e:["Correct. The CA revokes certificates and publishes the Certificate Revocation List. OCSP offers real-time status.","Incorrect. Key escrow holds copies of keys for recovery. It is unrelated to certificate status.","Incorrect. The subscriber requests certificates by sending a CSR. It cannot revoke them on the CA's behalf.","Incorrect. The RA vets identities on behalf of the CA but does not revoke certificates or publish the list."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"A browser checks the status of a single certificate in real time instead of downloading a full list. What is it using?",c:["CRL, a downloadable list of all revoked serial numbers","CSR, a request containing the public key","OCSP, an on-demand status lookup for one certificate","HSM, tamper-resistant key storage hardware"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A CRL is the full list, which the stem says the browser avoids. It would be the answer if the browser downloaded and cached the list.","Incorrect. A CSR is submitted to a CA when requesting a certificate. It reports no status.","Correct. OCSP returns the status of one certificate on request. OCSP stapling lets the server supply a signed response.","Incorrect. An HSM stores and protects keys. It does not answer revocation queries."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"A company wants one certificate for www, mail and shop under bayridge.com, and plans to add more subdomains over time. Which is the MOST efficient choice?",c:["Self-signed certificate generated by each server","Three separate EV certificates, one per hostname","Root CA certificate installed on each web server","Wildcard certificate for *.bayridge.com"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Self-signed certificates are not trusted by public browsers and would trigger warnings. They suit internal testing only.","Incorrect. This works but requires purchasing and renewing a new certificate for every added name. It is inefficient given planned growth.","Incorrect. A root certificate is the trust anchor of a CA hierarchy and is not installed on web servers for TLS.","Correct. A wildcard covers every subdomain one level below bayridge.com, so new names need no new certificate."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Which component is a dedicated chip on a laptop's motherboard that stores keys, records boot measurements and helps release the BitLocker key?",c:["HSM appliance in a network rack","Secure enclave inside the processor","TPM chip soldered to the mainboard","Key management service in the cloud"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. An HSM is a separate, high-performance appliance for enterprise key operations. It is not a chip on a laptop motherboard.","Incorrect. A secure enclave is an isolated area within a processor. It is similar in purpose but is part of the CPU, not a separate board chip.","Correct. A TPM is a hardware chip on the board that stores keys, measures boot integrity and unseals the disk encryption key only when the platform state is expected.","Incorrect. A cloud KMS is a service for managing keys at scale. It does not measure a laptop's boot process."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"A bank must perform thousands of signing operations per second with keys that never leave tamper-resistant hardware. Which is BEST?",c:["TPM on each signing server","Network HSM cluster in the data center","Software keystore encrypted with a master password","USB smart card token held by an officer"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A TPM protects one machine and is not designed for high-throughput enterprise signing. It would suit a per-device key such as BitLocker.","Correct. HSMs are dedicated, tamper-resistant devices built for high-volume cryptographic operations and secure key storage.","Incorrect. A software keystore keeps keys in memory or on disk where they can be extracted. It is not tamper-resistant hardware.","Incorrect. A smart card protects an individual's key but processes operations slowly and one at a time. It suits personal authentication."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Comparing the hash of a downloaded ISO with the hash published on the vendor's site primarily verifies which property?",c:["Integrity, since a match shows the file is unmodified","Availability, since the download is guaranteed to finish","Confidentiality, since the contents stay hidden","Non-repudiation, since the vendor signed the file"],a:0,e:["Correct. If the computed hash matches the published hash, the file was not altered or corrupted in transit.","Incorrect. A hash comparison has nothing to do with uptime or access. It would not make the download succeed.","Incorrect. Hashing does not hide anything. Encrypting the ISO would provide confidentiality.","Incorrect. A plain hash is not a signature, so it cannot bind the file to the vendor. A digitally signed hash would provide non-repudiation."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"A company wants to encrypt only the HR directory on a shared drive and leave the rest of the drive unencrypted. Which is MOST appropriate?",c:["Folder-level encryption such as EFS","Volume encryption of the shared drive","Full-disk encryption using BitLocker","Transport encryption with TLS 1.3"],a:0,e:["Correct. File- and folder-level encryption targets selected data, such as the HR directory, and leaves everything else alone.","Incorrect. Volume encryption covers an entire logical volume, not one directory. It would be right if the whole shared volume needed protection.","Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects everything on the drive, including data the company wants to leave unencrypted. It would fit a laptop theft scenario.","Incorrect. TLS protects data in transit, not stored files. It would apply to traffic between clients and the file server."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Encrypting only the D: portion of a disk while C: remains unencrypted is called what?",c:["File encryption, protecting individual documents","Partition encryption, protecting one slice of a disk","Transport encryption, protecting data in motion","Record encryption, protecting individual database rows"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. File encryption protects selected files, not a whole disk section. It would fit encrypting a single spreadsheet.","Correct. Partition encryption protects a defined partition such as D: while leaving other partitions as they are.","Incorrect. Transport encryption protects data moving across a network, not data stored on a disk.","Incorrect. Record-level encryption applies to rows or fields within a database."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"An attacker hides stolen data inside the pixels of JPEG images posted on social media. Which technique is this?",c:["Tokenization, replacing data with vault-mapped tokens","Masking, hiding part of a value such as ***-**-1234","Steganography, hiding data inside other content","Salting, adding random data before hashing"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Tokenization swaps sensitive values for tokens mapped in a secure vault. Nothing is hidden within another file.","Incorrect. Masking obscures part of a displayed value. It does not embed data inside other media.","Correct. Steganography hides the very existence of data inside carrier files such as images or audio.","Incorrect. Salting is a hashing technique for passwords and has no role in hiding data in images."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"A company keeps a copy of every employee's encryption key with a trusted third party so data can be recovered if an employee leaves. What is this?",c:["Key stretching, iterating a hash to slow brute force","Key exchange, agreeing on a key across an open channel","Certificate pinning, binding an app to one certificate","Key escrow, keeping a copy with a trusted third party"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Key stretching strengthens weak passwords or keys by repeated hashing. It does not store copies.","Incorrect. Key exchange establishes a shared key between parties, such as with Diffie-Hellman. It is not storage for recovery.","Incorrect. Pinning prevents an application from accepting unexpected certificates. It has nothing to do with data recovery.","Correct. Key escrow stores key copies with a trusted party so authorized recovery is possible when the original is unavailable."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Which describes an open public ledger?",c:["Centralized database controlled by a single large bank","List of revoked certificates published by a CA","Distributed append-only record shared by many parties","Escrow database holding copies of private keys"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. A ledger under one party's control is the model that an open ledger avoids. It would be a traditional database.","Incorrect. A list of revoked certificates is a CRL, which is controlled by the CA.","Correct. A blockchain is a distributed, append-only record in which each block is linked by hashes, so tampering is evident and no single party controls it.","Incorrect. Key escrow stores recovery keys and is not a transaction record."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"A developer wants each database row encrypted under its own key so one compromised key exposes only one row. Which level is this?",c:["Record-level encryption, using separate keys per row or field","Volume encryption, protecting a whole logical volume","Full-disk encryption, protecting the entire physical drive","Database-level encryption, using one key hierarchy for all data"],a:0,e:["Correct. Record-level encryption encrypts individual rows or fields, limiting exposure from a single key, at the cost of query performance.","Incorrect. Volume encryption covers everything on a logical volume with one key. It is not granular to rows.","Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects the whole drive and offers no per-row separation.","Incorrect. Database-level encryption such as TDE protects the entire database under one key hierarchy, so one compromised key exposes everything."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Which option is an example of key stretching?",c:["AES-256 with a randomly generated key","RSA with a 4096-bit modulus","Elliptic curve key pair on curve P-384","PBKDF2 using 600,000 HMAC iterations"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. AES-256 is a cipher with a long key. A long random key is not stretching because stretching strengthens a weak secret.","Incorrect. A larger RSA modulus lengthens a key but does not make a weak password harder to guess through repetition.","Incorrect. An elliptic curve key pair is an asymmetric algorithm choice. It does not stretch a password or passphrase.","Correct. Key stretching algorithms like PBKDF2 and bcrypt run many iterations so each password guess is expensive."]},
{o:"1.4",q:"Which certificate type gives visitors the HIGHEST assurance that the business behind the site was vetted by the CA?",c:["Domain validation (DV), after proof of control over the domain","Extended validation (EV), after rigorous organization vetting","Self-signed, issued by the server to itself","Wildcard, covering every subdomain at one level"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. DV only shows the requester controls the domain. It says nothing about the organization behind it.","Correct. EV requires the CA to perform the most thorough checks of the organization, which gives the highest identity assurance.","Incorrect. A self-signed certificate has no third-party verification of anything.","Incorrect. Wildcard describes how many names a certificate covers, not how rigorously the owner was vetted."]}
]);
